<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Four Books and Five Classics</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Four Books, Five Classics, Sìshū, Wǔjīng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Four Books and Five Classics are authoritative books associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, written before 300 BCE and traditionally believed to have been written, edited or commented on by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> or his disciples. The Five Classics, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Book of Documents</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/i-ching">I Ching</a> and the Spring and Autumn Annals, became part of the state curriculum under the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. The Four Books, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a>, were selected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> as an introduction to Confucian thought. In the Ming and Qing dynasties they formed the core of the civil service examinations.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Four Books and Five Classics are traditionally believed to have been written, edited or commented on by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> or one of his disciples. Several of the Five Classics were already prominent in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>. Under the Western Han dynasty, which adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> as its official ideology, the texts became part of the state-sponsored curriculum, and it was then that they were first considered together as the Five Classics. The Four Books were selected much later, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a>, as a general introduction to Confucian thought.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Five Classics are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a>, a collection of 305 poems; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Book of Documents</a>, speeches alleged to come from rulers and officials of the early Zhou period and before; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-rites">Book of Rites</a>, on ancient rites and court ceremonies; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/i-ching">I Ching</a>, a divination system; and the Spring and Autumn Annals, a record of the State of Lu for 722–481 BCE. A sixth, the Classic of Music, was lost.</p><p>The Four Books are the Great Learning, originally a chapter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-rites">Book of Rites</a>; the Doctrine of the Mean, another chapter of it, attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>'s grandson Zisi; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a>, speeches of Confucius and his disciples; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a>, long dialogues between Mencius and the kings of his time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Traditionally it was thought that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> compiled or edited the Five Classics, and that he cut the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classic-of-poetry">Classic of Poetry</a> from 3,000 pieces to 305. Many twentieth-century scholars hold that he had nothing to do with editing the classics, while others take the view that he and his followers contributed to their formation. For over 2,000 years he was believed to have written or edited them.</p><p>The Han adoption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> as state orthodoxy led to their preservation, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> revival made them the basis of orthodoxy in the examinations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> (1130–1200) fixed the texts of the Four Books and wrote commentaries whose interpretations came to be accepted as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> himself. In the Ming and Qing dynasties the Four Books formed the core of the civil service examinations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Four Books and Five Classics” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The classics include the Confucian <b>Four Books and Five Classics</b> and the Classic of Filial Piety, the Mozi, the Huainanzi and the Xunzi; in the Han dynasty Dong Zhongshu compiled the “Interactions Between Heaven and Mankind”.</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Its oldest layer is the <b>Five Classics</b>, works of different ages that tradition linked to Confucius as their editor or author: the Book of Songs, a collection of ancient poems; the Book of Documents, speeches and records of early kings; the Book of Rites, on ceremony and conduct;…</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Its foundations are the <b>Five Classics</b>, among them the Book of Songs, the oldest collection of Chinese poetry, and the Book of Changes, and the works of the philosophers, among them Confucius, Mencius, Laozi, Zhuangzi, Mozi and Han Feizi, and the Art of War of Sunzi.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Han Wudi (141–87 BCE), when the government began promoting Confucian studies, only the <b>Five Classics</b> were canonical, and the Analects was thought merely a collection of Confucius's oral commentary on them.</blockquote><a href="/work/analects">Analects · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>It joined the <b>Five Classics</b>, and the official edition was set in stone as one of the Xiping Stone Classics.</blockquote><a href="/work/i-ching">I Ching · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
